WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.635
-I'm always fascinated
to talk to mayors,

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.770
because, obviously, we spend
a lot of time on this show

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.838
talking about national politics.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.539
And the people
there spend a lot of time

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:07.640
in their home districts

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.142
but, obviously,
a lot of time in D.C.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:12.278
You spend all your time
living where you serve.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:13.813
You live amongst
your constituents.

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:15.348
Are people always
coming up to you

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.449
and telling them their problems?

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:17.650
-Of course.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.518
It's the most direct
democracy you can have.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:21.421
I mean, people find you
at the grocery store.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.789
People find you at the park.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.457
I mean, they'll come up
to you and tell you anything.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:26.258
You're, like, kind of
a confessor and a priest.

00:00:26.258 --> 00:00:29.062
They'll say like, "Hey, Mayor,
I had a great date last night."

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:30.663
You're like, "Awesome!"

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.366
Or somebody will come up -- "I
had a terrible date last night."

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.500
"Well, let's talk about that.
Why?"

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.836
-Yeah. Did you run on
a dating platform?

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.105
-I did. I did.
-Yeah. Okay.

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.239
-That can be taken two ways,
but I'll...

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.640
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.911
-No, I basically also have
my parents as my constituents.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.712
So, you know, if you've had
a Jewish mother ever before --

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.114
Like, it's like
I'm at a community meeting.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.948
They're like,
"The traffic's really bad

00:00:49.948 --> 00:00:51.150
on the West Side of L.A."

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.119
I say, "I know.
My mom called me yesterday.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.687
She had trouble
getting to my dad."

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.422
And, so, it's a
very accountable thing.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:59.558
But, all joking aside, it's also
where politics still lives.

00:00:59.558 --> 00:01:01.393
It's a place where
you can listen to people,

00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:03.663
solve their problems, and
actually accomplish something,

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.998
which is so different
than what we see in Washington.

00:01:05.998 --> 00:01:07.367
And that's not unique to L.A.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.469
That's every local community,
I think, where we live.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:11.604
So, you know, don't give up
on American politics

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.773
just because of what
you hear coming out of D.C.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:18.010
-And do you think, in general,
there's more optimism and...

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.079
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:22.014
There's a better chance for
optimism and change

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:24.083
on a local level than
there is on a national level.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:25.117
-Yeah. And, you know,

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.619
most politics happens
locally, anyway.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.588
I mean, it didn't
matter whether it was gonna be

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.190
President Trump
or President Clinton.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.258
Most of the work
you're gonna do on environment

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.326
is in your own backyard.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.161
Most of the work
you're gonna do on jobs,

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.296
on education, on your
commute and transportation.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:38.631
And people forget that,

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.801
because I think we cede a lot of
power to Washington, D.C.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.736
But look at
these kids in Florida.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:45.104
Like, that's
their local community.

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:46.972
There's more leadership
coming out of our teenagers

00:01:46.972 --> 00:01:49.475
than coming out of
Washington, D.C.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:52.311
And I also think
it's practical leadership.

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:53.812
People have strong beliefs
in our cities,

00:01:53.812 --> 00:01:54.914
our local communities,

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.215
but they also want
to see the job get done.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:57.216
They won't settle just for

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.886
a tweet, a re-tweet,
a counter-tweet.

00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:01.821
They want us to actually fix
the street, like, do something.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:02.989
-Yeah.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:04.490
-And then the nice thing
about being a mayor --

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.791
if I don't fix it,
I'm on that pothole.

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:07.193
-So more streets, less tweets?

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.694
-Absolutely.

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:10.529
[ Chanting ]
More streets, less tweets.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.231
More streets...
I like that.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.600
-Well, of course,
one of the problems in L.A. --

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.035
You mentioned potholes.

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:17.670
Obviously,
traffic's a huge deal.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.672
You guys have
the Olympics coming in 2028.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:22.308
And I feel like this is
the first time in a long time

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.744
that I've heard
someone actually got a good deal

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.312
as a host city for the Olympics.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.081
And you have an initiative
called Twenty-eight for '28.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:29.149
-Yes.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.483
-Is this about
public transportation

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.717
or just general traffic?

00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:33.185
-Yeah, well,
we're the, unfortunately,

00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:36.456
not only the car capital but
the traffic capital of America.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.458
But voters -- The same
night that President Trump

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.760
was elected, there was
other stuff on the ballot.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.062
And we passed
a $120 billion initiative

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:47.433
to build 15 transit lines in
L.A., where cars reign supreme.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.668
And by '28 -- 2028 --
when the Olympics come,

00:02:49.668 --> 00:02:52.138
we'll have 28 of
those projects done that are

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.340
improvements of roads,
new lines.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.109
A lot of middle-class jobs,
too, that you can't export.

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:58.778
And I think people
talk about politics --

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.146
they say words
like "infrastructure."

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.280
What does that mean?

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.249
It means me getting home
to my wife, my daughter.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:06.786
It means me deciding I can make
it to that date on time,

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:08.053
take that job.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:09.889
We forgot to talk about
things in human terms.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:12.191
So, we're accelerating all this
cool stuff for the Olympics.

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:14.360
I'm really proud that America
will get the Olympics back

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.230
for the Summer Games,
the first time since 1996.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.631
And we're gonna
make money off of it,

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.767
'cause we have everything
basically built in L.A.,

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.769
from the Rams' stadium
that's gonna be coming up,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.970
using UCLA as our
Olympic Village.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:28.140
So we can focus on providing
sports to a bunch of kids

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.476
leading up to then and after.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.278
-That's really exciting.
-Yeah.

